                        "answer": "For a JavaScript website, **prerendering** means generating a page’s HTML ahead of time—at build time or on the server—so the browser receives meaningful content immediately instead of an almost-empty HTML shell that must run JavaScript first. JavaScript can then “hydrate” that HTML to add interactivity. This can speed up the initial display and help crawlers that don’t execute JavaScript; Google recommends server-side or static rendering over dynamic rendering as a workaround. ([developers.goog",
